    <!-- Solutions (per-probe "how to fix", assigned manually) -->
    <string name="sol_title">How to fix</string>
    <string name="card_details">Details</string>
    <string name="sol_boot">Hardware attestation (TEE) reports the boot state. Add this app package to your keybox tool\'s target list so it reports a locked, verified device.</string>
    <string name="sol_attest_consistency">These probes cross-check two attestation paths that must describe the same device: the EC chain against the RSA chain, the native keystore2 chain against the managed one, or the leaf signature against the expected keybox type. A mismatch means one path was rewritten while the other was not, or a shared keybox is in use. Point your keybox tool at every attestation path so all of them stay consistent.</string>
    <string name="sol_secure_hw">The key should live in secure hardware: the TEE, or a StrongBox secure element, which rejects key parameters it does not support. A software security level, or a StrongBox that accepts unsupported parameters, means the secure hardware is emulated. Use a device with genuine secure hardware, or a tool that models it faithfully.</string>
    <string name="sol_prov_vbmeta_reuse">The RKP vbmeta_digest (from the RKP certificate) does not match the device runtime verified boot hash, so the keybox was provisioned on a different device (a reused or shared keybox) or the device was updated after provisioning. Use a keybox provisioned on this device.</string>
    <string name="sol_prov_fingerprint">The RKP fingerprint (from the RKP certificate) does not match this device build fingerprint, so the keybox belongs to a different device. Use a keybox provisioned on this device.</string>
    <string name="sol_device_id">The attestation hardware identity (brand, device, manufacturer) does not match the build properties, so a verifier can tell the properties are spoofed. Align ro.product.brand, ro.product.device and ro.product.manufacturer with the hardware-attested values (product and model legitimately differ: the attestation carries the codename).</string>
    <string name="sol_prov_verdict">The RKP certificate records the device boot as not approved, so the RKP backend flagged this device. Use a keybox provisioned on a device the backend approved.</string>
    <string name="sol_root_detector">These installed apps look for root. Add the packages found here to your hiding framework (HMA-OSS) as targets.</string>
    <string name="sol_spoof">This setting does not read consistently. Add it to the HMA-OSS spoof presets so every path returns the same value.</string>
    <string name="sol_attest_patch">Attestation reports a different security patch than the property. Set it in your keybox tool (TrickyStore security_patch.txt, OhMyKeymint config.toml [trust]); it changes only attestation.</string>
    <string name="sol_patch_prop">The framework and the property show different patch dates. Align them with resetprop; keybox tools change only attestation, not properties.</string>
    <string name="note_art_dexopt_self">Health signal, not a root tell. An AOT base.odex exists on disk but the app runs interpreted (no executable mapping), meaning the odex was rejected (boot-image out of date, the ART boot image changed after an apex update). Service apps and Play Protect app verification can then fail to start in time. User-side proxy; the authoritative check is root-side.</string>
    <string name="sol_art_dexopt_self">Re-compile as root (pm compile -a -m speed) or reboot to regenerate the boot image and re-dexopt the apps.</string>
    <string name="sol_datadir_errno_oracle">A stat on a package\'s data directory can reveal it as installed even with no package query. Add such packages to your hiding framework (HMA-OSS) so their data directories read as absent, not merely denied.</string>
    <string name="sol_signing_keys">A production build is signed with release-keys. A signing-key property reading test-keys or dev-keys (by any method) exposes a custom or debug build. Restore release-keys across build.tags and the per-partition *.keys properties.</string>
    <string name="sol_lock_coherence">On a stock locked device every bootloader-lock and verified-boot property agrees: flash.locked=1, verifiedbootstate=green, vbmeta.device_state=locked, warranty untripped. When one claims locked while another still reads unlocked, a spoof patched some but not all of them. Normalize every lock property to the same state.</string>
    <string name="sol_atexit_drift">The libc exit handlers __cxa_atexit and __cxa_finalize must resolve inside libc. When they resolve into another library or an anonymous region, a Zygisk-style injector interposed them. Remove the injection from this process.</string>
    <string name="sol_zygote_gid">The zygote assigns a process its supplementary GIDs from the app\'s granted permissions: INTERNET implies 3003, BLUETOOTH 3002, and so on. A granted permission whose GID is absent from getgroups(2) means a hider filtered the zygote permissions: it hides the app but contradicts the permission set, which any app reads on itself. Do not strip GIDs that granted permissions require (for example, never remove INET_GID while INTERNET is granted).</string>
    <string name="sol_perm_path_coherence">The same permission is resolved by several code paths: PackageManager.checkPermission, Context.checkPermission(perm,pid,uid), the granted flags of getPackageInfo(GET_PERMISSIONS), and the low-level IPackageManager.checkUidPermission binder. On an untouched system all reachable paths agree, and a privileged permission (START_ANY_ACTIVITY, INSTALL_PACKAGES, MANAGE_USERS) is denied on every one. A hook that forges or hides a permission on one path but not another, or that grants a privileged permission a normal app can never hold, diverges here. Resolve permissions consistently across every path; do not spoof a grant on only the path a detector happens to read.</string>
    <string name="sol_baseband_coherence">The modem version is reported by getRadioVersion and the gsm.version.baseband family alike; they all read the same firmware. When one source disagrees, a property spoof patched some aliases but not all. Keep every baseband version source identical.</string>
    <string name="sol_fingerprint_rebuild">The build fingerprint is composed at build time as BRAND/PRODUCT/DEVICE:RELEASE/ID/INCREMENTAL:TYPE/TAGS. When Build.FINGERPRINT does not equal that reconstruction from its own component fields, a spoof changed the fingerprint without aligning every component (or the reverse). Make the fingerprint and its components consistent.</string>
    <string name="sol_signature_source">The signing certificate read from the PackageManager record and the one parsed from the APK file on disk must match, and the PackageManager must recognise the APK-file certificate (hasSigningCertificate, checkSignatures). When they diverge, a PackageManager-level hook rewrote the reported signature without touching the actual APK. A direct APK read still sees the real certificate.</string>
    <string name="sol_signature_sweep">For every installed app, the signing certificate from the PackageManager record and the one parsed from its APK file share a signer. An app whose two sets are disjoint has had its PackageManager signature spoofed while the APK on disk kept the real one. Key-rotation keeps a common signer, so it is not flagged.</string>
    <string name="sol_hidden_app_usage_leak">A package can surface through the usage-stats APIs even when it is kept out of the installed-package list. Add such packages to your hiding framework (HMA-OSS) so they are hidden from the usage-stats APIs too, not only from the package list.</string>
    <string name="sol_launch_sender_pkg_leak">On measured ROMs the returned IntentSender is keyed only by (caller, name): its creator fields are the caller\'s and equals differs by the name alone, so it carries no installed/hidden signal (comparing against a random absent name is a false positive). It only leaks if a visible installed package and an absent name differ by a caller-independent field; if so, make the hiding framework (HMA-OSS) return the absent-equivalent for hidden packages.</string>
    <string name="sol_phantom_monitor">A stock device does not expose whether the phantom-process monitor was changed. Add the setting to your hiding framework (HMA-OSS) spoof presets so it reads as on a stock device, and avoid the feature-flag override property.</string>
    <string name="sol_otacerts_signing_key">The OTA signing certificate (otacerts.zip) is the public AOSP test key, so the build reads as test-signed even when ro.build.tags claims release-keys. Serve a release-signed otacerts.zip through your file-redirection module and align ro.*.build.tags across every partition so the file and the properties agree.</string>
    <string name="sol_platform_signer">The platform (android) package signature reveals a non-OEM signer (a custom ROM or the AOSP test key) or an OEM that does not match the claimed manufacturer. Spoof the platform signature (as PlayIntegrityFork\'s spoofSignature does) so the android package reports the claimed OEM\'s platform certificate.</string>
    <string name="sol_libc_identity">The loaded libc mapping (device and inode) does not match the libc.so file on disk, so the library was replaced or bind-mounted (a hooked libc). Serve the genuine libc, or hide the replacement mount from the app.</string>
    <string name="sol_statfs_consistency">android.os.StatFs and the native statvfs reading disagree on storage size, so the size is spoofed through the Java framework API but not natively. Make the StatFs and native paths report the same values.</string>
    <string name="sol_feature_spoof">hasSystemFeature, getSystemAvailableFeatures and the hardware nodes disagree about a device feature, so a feature is hidden or faked through one path but not the others. Make every feature-query path and the hardware report the same presence.</string>
