    <string name="sol_loop_orphan">On a stock device every ext4 loop is backed by a system mount in this namespace. A loop with no system mount point is a module or overlay mounted outside the stock layout. Mount such loops under a system path, or keep them in a mount namespace this app does not share.</string>
    <string name="sol_debug_ramdisk">On a stock boot /debug_ramdisk is empty or absent. Files staged there are an early-boot root or module payload. Clear /debug_ramdisk of staged content, or keep it in a namespace this app cannot read.</string>
    <string name="sol_stock_path_hidden">A standard Android system path is present on a stock device, so a stock path that reads as absent means a hiding layer is over-hiding it, which is itself a tell. Hide only root and module paths, never a path that exists on a clean device.</string>
    <string name="sol_rom_identity">On an honest device the SoC, baseband and init identity match the declared brand and model. When a ROM or hardware identity property contradicts the claimed device, a fingerprint spoof covered the product props but not the hardware ones. Keep every identity surface (SoC, baseband, init.svc, cpuinfo) consistent with the spoofed device.</string>
    <string name="sol_version_cluster">A stock build agrees with itself about its release state: codename, release-or-preview SDK and fingerprint token all read release, or all read preview, never a mix. When one signal reads release while another reads preview, a spoof changed some version props but not all. Align every version-state signal to the same channel.</string>
    <string name="sol_multiuser_oracle">A secondary user or private space leaks when a stat on /data/user/&lt;id&gt; reads it as present while UserManager does not list it. Make the two agree: either keep the user data directory unreachable to an unprivileged app, or do not hide a user that its own directory still reveals.</string>
    <string name="sol_prov_manufacturer">The RKP manufacturer (from the RKP certificate) does not match this device, so the keybox was provisioned for a different device. Use a keybox provisioned on this device.</string>
